From 5f7a5f329aaee87de792b49b03d09a74b52f0aa1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 01:03:14 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the television depth from measuring the player's body

The depth test learns what the game's depth values mean (B in device = A + B / z,
the camera's near plane) from the screens themselves. It sampled a few points in
the middle of each screen's glass and took the smallest of device depth times the
screen's view depth, on the grounds that anything in front of the glass only
makes that product larger.

In game that broke. A 100 ft cinema set up in an earlier session had no panel in
the game's depth behind its picture, so the points saw the sky -- except where the
player's own body was in front of them, in third person. Those groups agreed with
each other while the player stood watching, and the calibration locked on them:
0.16 to 0.27 instead of the 0.0200 every earlier session measured, so the picture
counted as about ten times nearer than it was and was drawn over the player at
anything closer than 25 to 40 m. It came back once the player walked far enough
away, and went again on the way back. A newly placed cinema later in the same
session measured 0.01997 again.

A body, a hand or a wall in front of a screen stays put in front of the camera
while the screen does not, so what it says follows the screen's distance; the
glass's value does not. The calibration now:

  * counts a group only when at least four of its points agree (to 2 %) on the
    lowest value they share, so a lone point past an edge, or points spread over
    a body, are not a value at all;
  * takes a value only from groups that agree AND saw their screens from
    distances at least 1.2 times apart (the farthest over the nearest of the
    groups that agree), which nothing standing in front of a screen can do;
  * starts reversed depth from this game's near plane, 0.02 m (every lock
    measured in game: 0.01985 to 0.01998), so the test is right from the first
    frame instead of off until something is measured -- and a screen with no
    panel cannot talk it into anything else. A measurement replaces it, and
    later moves still need a whole window.

The log says "assumed what the depth means" at the start, and the distances the
screens were measured from when a measurement replaces it; the status line and
the debug view mark an assumed value.

Open77.SceneDepthPolicy.Tests pins the session that showed it (a torso 2.6 m from
the camera in front of a panel-less screen, standing for four windows and walking
from 40 m to 10 m: the near plane stays), a real near-plane change seen from one
distance (not taken) and from several (taken), points that disagree, a point past
an edge, and the sky far behind a screen.

